## Founding & History

Bream Creek Vineyard is recognized as one of Tasmania's pioneering and oldest commercial vineyards. The vineyard was originally planted in either 1973 or 1974 on Tasmania's east coast. The initial planting covered 5 hectares and primarily featured Riesling and Cabernet Sauvignon, alongside smaller plots of Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, and Gewürztraminer.

The vineyard was initially owned by a wine consortium or a syndicate of business operators, which included Dr. Bob Menary. In August 1990, the property was purchased by Fred Peacock. Fred Peacock established the Bream Creek wine label in the same year, 1990. At the time of his acquisition, there were fewer than 10 commercial wineries operating in Tasmania. Fred Peacock was the successful bidder when the previous owners decided to auction the property. He had previously managed the Bream Creek Vineyard from early 1989 to late 1992, during which time Moorilla was a partner in the vineyard.

A significant milestone for the winery was the official opening of its new cellar door in early August, by Tasmanian Premier Jeremy Rockliff.

## People

The current owner and viticulturist of Bream Creek Vineyard is Fred Peacock. He is frequently described as a "renowned Tasmanian vigneron", a "viticulturist extraordinaire and legend", and a "legend on the Tasmanian wine scene".

Fred Peacock holds a Degree in Agricultural Science from the University of Tasmania. His career in viticulture began in the mid-1970s when he was appointed as a Horticultural Officer with the Department of Agriculture. He later became Tasmania's first Viticultural Officer in the early 1980s. Prior to owning Bream Creek, he worked at Moorilla Estate (now the site of MONA) as vineyard manager when it was owned by Claudio Alcorso, and later as general manager from early 1989 until late 1992.

The wines of Bream Creek are made by Julian Alcorso and his team at Winemaking Tasmania, a contract-crush facility. Julian Alcorso is the son of Claudio Alcorso.

## Vineyard & Site

Bream Creek Vineyard is situated on Tasmania's east coast, offering views over Marion Bay and the Tasman Sea, as well as Maria Island. The vineyard is planted on a gently sloping site.

The vineyard's current size is reported as 7.5 hectares or 7.6 hectares. The original planting in the early 1970s covered 5 hectares. Initially, the vineyard was planted with Riesling, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, and Gewürztraminer. Upon purchasing the vineyard in 1990, Fred Peacock increased the plantings of Pinot Noir. He also introduced Sauvignon Blanc and Schönburger. The Schönburger planting at Bream Creek was among the earliest commercial plantings of this rare German varietal in the Southern Hemisphere, with Fred Peacock believing it to be the first.

Fred Peacock removed approximately 40 percent of the original Cabernet Sauvignon plantings, along with other minor varieties such as Alicante Bouschet, Müller-Thurgau, and Pinot Blanc. Some Riesling vines at Bream Creek are over 40 years old, making them among the oldest Riesling vines in Tasmania.

The vineyard's soils are deep, ranging from brown to black clay loams with some small stones. The climate is described as cool maritime, characterized by long, mild seasons, cool frost-free springs, sunny summer days moderated by gentle sea breezes, and an extended autumn ripening period that enhances flavor development. The surrounding topography and forest provide a natural windbreak.

Farming practices at Bream Creek Vineyard involve meticulous management of the vines using vertical-shoot positioning. Most operations, including pruning and picking, are performed by hand.

## Cellar Door Experience

Bream Creek Vineyard has a cellar door located at the top of the vineyard, offering views over Marion Bay and Maria Island. The cellar door is open from Wednesday to Monday, from 10:30 AM to 5:00 PM. Bookings are recommended via their website to avoid disappointment. Visitors can enjoy locally sourced cheese and meat platters, and Tassie Gold oysters, while tasting a range of Bream Creek wines.

## Food & Hospitality

The cellar door offers locally sourced cheese and meat platters, and Tassie Gold oysters. No reliable information found from public sources regarding a full restaurant or kitchen, menu style, group capacity for separate events beyond the cellar door offerings.

## Awards & Recognition

Bream Creek wines have been consistent award winners for over 30 years. Since 1990, the vineyard has accumulated 18 trophies and over 400 show medals. Since 2010, Bream Creek Vineyard has been rated as a red five-star winery by the James Halliday Wine Companion. Since 1990, the winery has been rewarded with 55 trophies and more than 1,000 show medals.

Specific awards mentioned include the 2017 Schönburger winning the Stewards' Choice Trophy at Mildura's Australian Alternative Varieties Wine Show in 2018. In 2014, Bream Creek took home 11 medals at the Tasmanian Wine Show, including five gold medals (two top-of-class), one silver, and five bronze. Top wines included their 2007 sparkling wine, 2012 riesling, 2011 riesling, 2012 VGR (medium-sweet) riesling, and 2012 pinot noir (all gold), while its 2012 chardonnay received a high silver.

Critic scores include:
* Bream Creek Brut Rosé NV: 96 Points
* Bream Creek Chardonnay 2019: 93 Points
* Bream Creek Chardonnay 2024: 92 silver
* Bream Creek Lieblich Field Blend 2024: 97 Points
* Bream Creek Late Picked Schönburger 2023 375ml: 93 Points
* Bream Creek Pinot Noir 2024 Tasmania: 92 silver
* Bream Creek Riesling 2024: 93 Points
* Bream Creek Sauvignon Blanc 2025: 91 Points

